(window.FAIAS_DOC=window.FAIAS_DOC||{})["engagement-model"]={"html":"<h1>ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md — FAIAS Assurance Reviewer's Handbook</h1>\n<p><strong>Version:</strong> the release version in <code>README.md</code>; the signed manifest, <code>ownership/MANIFEST.sha256</code>, identifies the release.<br />\n<strong>Document Custodian:</strong> the maintainer</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Purpose</h2>\n<p>This document is the operational handbook for reviewers (assurance practitioners, internal audit, external auditors) conducting a FAIAS engagement. It defines six phases, deliverables per phase, governance decision-points, and tools to apply.</p>\n<p>A FAIAS engagement produces an Independent Assurance Opinion (Component 7) backed by systematic threat analysis, evidence collection, and claims assessment.</p>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Calibration note.</strong> Phase durations and procedure depth in this handbook describe the maximal, Tier 3 form of the engagement and assume manual execution. They are a ceiling to calibrate down from, not a required floor. Scope, depth, sample size and repetition are set per engagement against tier, consequence, evidence availability, the named expert's direction and the depth commissioned; automated test execution reduces effort on mechanical test classes substantially without changing the ★ confidence of the method. Every reduction is recorded, and anything not tested is dispositioned with its reason and consequence rather than omitted silently. See PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §5A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>FAIAS Engagement Model: Six Phases</h2>\n<pre><code>┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 1: Intake &amp; Scoping                           │\n│ Output: AI Deployment Profile (1-pager)             │\n│ Owner: Engagement Lead + Domain Expert              │\n│ Duration: 1–2 weeks                                 │\n│ Gate: Scope validated; AI Risk Tier tentative       │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 2: Materiality Assessment                     │\n│ Output: AI Risk Tier + Scoped IRQ Set               │\n│ Owner: Assurance Lead + Risk Architect (firm-side)  │\n│ Duration: 1 week                                    │\n│ Gate: Tier confirmed; engagement scope locked       │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 3: Threat &amp; Failure Analysis                 │\n│ Output: Prioritised IRQ Set + Failure Trees         │\n│ Owner: Joint workshop (domain expert + AI reviewer)  │\n│ Duration: 2–3 weeks                                 │\n│ Gate: Failure modes validated; IRQ list finalised   │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 4: Evidence &amp; Testing                         │\n│ Output: Test Results, Evidence Catalogue            │\n│ Owner: Testing Lead                                 │\n│ Duration: 4–9 weeks (depends on Tier; §6 stages)    │\n│ Gate: Testing complete; evidence levels assigned    │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 5: Claims Assessment &amp; Opinion                │\n│ Output: Independent Assurance Opinion               │\n│ Owner: Assurance Lead + Engagement Lead             │\n│ Duration: 2–3 weeks                                 │\n│ Gate: Opinion reviewed by QA; issued to the sponsor │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 6: Continuous Assurance &amp; Lifecycle           │\n│ Output: Monitoring Plan, Re-Assessment Schedule     │\n│ Owner: Assurance Lead + Operations (firm-side)      │\n│ Duration: Ongoing (design through retirement)       │\n│ Gate: Monitoring activated; lifecycle documented    │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h2>Phase 1: Intake &amp; Scoping</h2>\n<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Gather information about the AI system, its deployment context, and business/regulatory environment. Produce a one-page AI Deployment Profile for sponsor review.</p>\n<h3>Information Gathered</h3>\n<h4>Business Context</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use Case &amp; Business Objective:</strong> What decision or task does the AI support? What customer outcome is enabled? (e.g., \"Support credit underwriting decision for SME lending\")</li>\n<li><strong>Process &amp; Workflow:</strong> How does the AI output integrate into business process? Who reviews the AI output? Who is accountable for the decision? Is there a human-in-loop or is system autonomous?</li>\n<li><strong>Customer Impact:</strong> Which customers are affected? What's the materiality of the decision (eligibility, pricing, access to service)? Are vulnerable customers involved (disability, elderly, financial literacy)?</li>\n<li><strong>Regulatory Obligations:</strong> Which regulatory frameworks apply? (FCA SYSC 4.1, PRA model risk, Consumer Duty, AML/CFT, data protection, market conduct, etc.)</li>\n<li><strong>Accountable Executive:</strong> Who owns this system end-to-end? Who is the SMCR-accountable person for model risk governance?</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>AI System Context</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Model Type &amp; Provenance:</strong> Is this a large language model (LLM), supervised classifier, regression model, reinforcement learning agent, ensemble? Who built/trained it (internal, external vendor)? What version?</li>\n<li><strong>Fine-Tuning or Adaptation:</strong> Has the base model been fine-tuned? On what data? How recently? Who owns fine-tuning decisions?</li>\n<li><strong>Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG):</strong> If system uses retrieval, what are the data sources? How fresh are they? Who curates them?</li>\n<li><strong>Agents &amp; Tool Use:</strong> Does system have access to tools, APIs, or autonomous decision-making? What are the tools/decisions it can make?</li>\n<li><strong>Memory &amp; State:</strong> Does system maintain context across multiple requests? Is state persisted? Are there prompt injections/jailbreak risks?</li>\n<li><strong>Human Involvement:</strong> What does human reviewer do? Can human override? Is override logged and audited?</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Deployment Environment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Development Environment:</strong> Where is the system built? Access controls, version control, testing protocols</li>\n<li><strong>Test/Staging Environment:</strong> Is there a non-production environment? How representative is it of production?</li>\n<li><strong>Production Environment:</strong> Cloud provider? Self-hosted? Data residency? Incident response procedures?</li>\n<li><strong>Third-Party Dependencies:</strong> Model host (e.g., OpenAI API), data providers, infrastructure vendors. Who has data access? What are SLAs?</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring &amp; Alerting:</strong> What KPIs are tracked? Are there automated alerts? Who responds to incidents?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Deliverable: AI Deployment Profile (1-pager)</h3>\n<p><strong>Template:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nAI DEPLOYMENT PROFILE\nSystem: [Name]  |  Version: [X.Y]  |  Date: [YYYY-MM-DD]\n═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nBUSINESS CONTEXT\nUse Case: [One-sentence: what problem does it solve?]\nDecision Supported: [Who decides? Human or autonomous?]\nCustomer Impact: [Materiality: eligibility / pricing / access / none]\nRegulatory Framework: [FCA, PRA, GDPR, etc.]\nAccountable Executive: [Name, Title]\n\nAI SYSTEM\nModel Type: [LLM / classifier / agent / ensemble / other]\nProvider: [Internal / OpenAI / Anthropic / other]\nAdaptation: [Fine-tuned? RAG? Custom tools?]\nHuman Role: [Advisory / approver / exception handler / post-audit]\n\nDEPLOYMENT ENVIRONMENT\nStaging: [Yes/No — representative of production?]\nProduction: [Cloud provider / data residency]\nMonitoring: [KPIs tracked; alerting in place?]\nIncidents: [Last 12 months: [count] incidents; escalation procedure documented]\n\nPRELIMINARY RISK TIER (to be confirmed in Phase 2):\n[ ] Tier 0  [ ] Tier 1  [ ] Tier 2  [ ] Tier 3\n\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS FOR PHASE 2:\n1. [Domain expert question e.g., &quot;Are vulnerable customers affected?&quot;]\n2. [Reviewer question e.g., &quot;Is RAG data source controlled?&quot;]\n3. [Regulatory question e.g., &quot;Does PRA model risk governance apply?&quot;]\n\nPrepared by: [Name]  |  Sponsor Review: [ ] Approved\n═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Gate 1: Intake Complete</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Proceed to Phase 2 (Materiality Assessment)\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> Sponsor + Engagement Lead\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- Deployment Profile complete and sponsor-reviewed\n- Critical questions identified\n- No blockers to proceeding (e.g., no \"system not yet built\" or \"scope undefined\")</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Phase 2: Materiality Assessment</h2>\n<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Apply TIERING_MODEL.md to determine AI Risk Tier. Tier determines scope of IRQ assessment, evidence levels, opinion type, and engagement duration.</p>\n<h3>Process</h3>\n<p><strong>Step 1: Score Five Factors</strong>\n- Customer Impact (1–4)\n- Financial Impact (1–4)\n- Regulatory Impact (1–4)\n- Autonomy Level (0–5, normalized to 1–4)\n- System Complexity (0–5, normalized to 1–4)</p>\n<p>Use TIERING_MODEL.md definitions and worked examples; involve domain expert to ground scoring.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Calculate Composite Risk Score</strong></p>\n<pre><code>Risk Score = \n  (Customer Impact × 0.25) + \n  (Financial Impact × 0.25) + \n  (Regulatory Impact × 0.25) + \n  (Autonomy normalized × 0.125) + \n  (Complexity normalized × 0.125)\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Check Override Triggers</strong>\n- Vulnerable customer exposure? Bump up 1 tier.\n- Regulatory feedback history? Bump up 1 tier.\n- Emerging technology risk? Bump up 1 tier.\n- Other? (See TIERING_MODEL.md section: Override Triggers)</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Assign Tier</strong>\nMap Risk Score to tier (Tier 0–3 per TIERING_MODEL.md).</p>\n<h3>Deliverable: Materiality Assessment Summary</h3>\n<p>Document:\n- Five-factor scores with rationale per factor\n- Composite Risk Score calculation\n- Override conditions (if any)\n- Final Tier assignment\n- Scope of IRQ assessment (which domains in scope)\n- Engagement duration estimate\n- Evidence-level minimums per tier</p>\n<h3>Gate 2: Materiality Assessment &amp; Tier Locked</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Proceed to Phase 3 (Threat &amp; Failure Analysis)\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> Assurance Lead (or Sponsor for urgent engagements)\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- Tier assignment justified and documented\n- All five factors scored with rationale\n- Override triggers reviewed\n- Scope of IRQs locked (no future scope changes without re-tiering)\n- Engagement plan (duration, team, budget) approved</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Phase 3: Threat &amp; Failure Analysis</h2>\n<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Conduct a joint workshop with domain expert (business owner, process subject-matter expert) and AI reviewer to build failure trees: Business Objective → AI Function → AI Behaviour → Failure Modes → Consequences. This grounds IRQ prioritisation in business risk, not technical curiosity.</p>\n<h3>Workshop Structure</h3>\n<p><strong>Part A: Business Objectives (Domain Expert)</strong>\n- \"What are the 5–10 key decisions this process makes?\"\n- \"What are the worst outcomes if a decision is wrong?\"\n- \"Which decisions are most material to the business?\"\n- Example: Lending: \"Decisions: approve/decline/tier. Worst outcome: approve bad credit (loss). Material: yes.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Part B: AI Functions (AI Reviewer)</strong>\n- \"What does the AI do in the workflow?\"\n- \"What does the AI output look like? (score, classification, ranking, recommendation)\"\n- \"How does human use the output?\" (override, approve, exception-handle, accept as-is)\n- Example: \"System outputs credit score 650–850. Underwriter approves if &gt;700.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Part C: AI Behaviours (Joint)</strong>\n- \"What could the AI do differently than expected?\"\n- \"Are there edge cases where behaviour diverges from training?\"\n- Example: \"System hallucinates customer data from similar names. System lacks context on recent life events (job loss, bankruptcy).\"</p>\n<p><strong>Part D: Failure Modes (AI Reviewer)</strong>\n- \"How does behaviour cross the unsafe boundary?\"\n- \"What are the ways confidence becomes unsafe?\"\n- Example: \"System confidently recommends approval for customer with recent CCJ (confident hallucination of creditworthiness).\"</p>\n<p><strong>Part E: Consequences (Domain Expert)</strong>\n- \"What is the business/customer/regulatory impact?\"\n- Example: \"Wrong approval → customer in unaffordable debt → regulatory breach (affordability) → FOS complaint → capital charge.\"</p>\n<h3>Deliverable: Failure Trees (Per Business Objective)</h3>\n<p><strong>Template:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>BUSINESS OBJECTIVE: Approve/decline credit for SME lending\n│\n├─ DECISION: Approve if system score &gt;750\n│  │\n│  ├─ AI BEHAVIOUR: System scores applicant based on financial history\n│  │  │\n│  │  ├─ FAILURE MODE: System hallucinates customer creditworthiness\n│  │  │                (confident score based on fabricated data)\n│  │  │  └─ CONSEQUENCE: Approve unaffordable loan → customer default\n│  │  │                  → regulatory breach → FOS complaint\n│  │  │                  → Tier: Customer impact 4, Financial 4, Regulatory 4\n│  │  │                  → IRQs: IRQ-MOD-004 (hallucination), IRQ-DAT-003 (data quality),\n│  │  │                          IRQ-HUM-004 (escalation), IRQ-CON-005 (financial exclusion)\n│  │  │\n│  │  └─ FAILURE MODE: System confidence miscalibrated\n│  │                   (score high but reliability low)\n│  │     └─ CONSEQUENCE: Approve borderline credit → customer struggle\n│  │                     → complaint → reputation damage\n│  │                     → IRQs: IRQ-MOD-006 (confidence calibration)\n│  │\n│  └─ FAILURE MODE: System over-relies on single weak signal\n│                   (e.g., recent industry switch)\n│     └─ CONSEQUENCE: Approve risky credit → loss → capital impact\n│                     → IRQs: IRQ-DAT-006 (ingestion and curation integrity), IRQ-MOD-010 (robustness to shift)\n│\n└─ DECISION: Decline if system score &lt;700\n   │\n   └─ FAILURE MODE: System cannot adapt to changed circumstances\n                    (customer recovering after historic default)\n      └─ CONSEQUENCE: Decline creditworthy applicant → customer friction\n                      → regulatory fairness concern (opportunity)\n                      → IRQs: IRQ-DAT-007 (retrieval currency), IRQ-CON-003 (fairness)\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Phase 3 Prioritisation</h3>\n<p>For each failure mode, assign <strong>priority</strong> based on:\n1. <strong>Severity:</strong> Impact on customer/firm (high/medium/low)\n2. <strong>Likelihood:</strong> How easily could this happen in production? (high/medium/low)\n3. <strong>Detectability:</strong> How easy is it to catch (with current controls)? (easy/medium/hard)</p>\n<p><strong>Priority = Severity + Likelihood − Detectability</strong> (conceptually; use FAIAS 5-axis risk weighting if finer granularity needed)</p>\n<h3>Deliverable: Prioritised IRQ Set</h3>\n<p><strong>Output:</strong>\n- List of IRQs in scope for this engagement (selected from the full FAIAS battery — 109 IRQs as at v1.2.0; see IRQ_REGISTER.md)\n- IRQs ranked by priority (high → medium → low)\n- Cross-domain chains identified (e.g., data poisoning → model hallucination → human accept → customer loss)</p>\n<h3>Gate 3: Failure Analysis &amp; IRQ Set Locked</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Proceed to Phase 4 (Evidence &amp; Testing)\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> Domain Expert + Assurance Lead\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- Failure trees documented (business objective → failure mode → consequence)\n- All critical failure modes identified\n- IRQ set prioritised\n- Cross-domain chains documented\n- No unaddressed failure modes</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Phase 4: Evidence &amp; Testing</h2>\n<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Execute non-production testing per Component 5 Testing Playbook, collect evidence per Component 4 Evidence Catalogue, and assign evidence levels (L1–L5) and testing confidence (★1–★5).</p>\n<h3>Process per Tier</h3>\n<p><strong>Tier 0:</strong>\n- Design review only (no testing)\n- Governance documentation audit (policy, roles)\n- Evidence level: L2</p>\n<p><strong>Tier 1:</strong>\n- Staged testing for priority IRQs (not exhaustive)\n- Evidence: L3 (design + demo)\n- Testing confidence: ★3–★4 where available; ★1–★2 procedures run with disclosure (PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §8)</p>\n<p><strong>Tier 2:</strong>\n- Comprehensive staged testing (all IRQs in scope)\n- Benchmarked test methods (OWASP, NIST)\n- Evidence: L4 (independent testing)\n- Testing confidence: ★4 or above on material tests for reasonable assurance; a material test rated ★1–★3 runs with disclosure, and the opinion is then given at limited assurance</p>\n<p><strong>Tier 3:</strong>\n- Exhaustive staged testing (all IRQs, multiple tests per IRQ)\n- L4 evidence from staging\n- L5 evidence post-deployment (operational validation)\n- Testing confidence: ★4 or above on all material tests for reasonable assurance; otherwise as Tier 2</p>\n<h3>Deliverable: Evidence Catalogue (Component 4)</h3>\n<p>Structured inventory of all evidence collected, per FAIAS template:\n- Policy &amp; governance documents\n- Technical design\n- Testing results (per IRQ, per test)\n- Monitoring data\n- Third-party attestations</p>\n<p><strong>Evidence tagging:</strong>\n- L1 (assertion), L2 (documentation), L3 (demo), L4 (independent test), L5 (operational)\n- Confidence rating (★1–★5)\n- Which claim(s) does evidence support?</p>\n<h3>Gate 4: Testing Complete &amp; Evidence Catalogued</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Proceed to Phase 5 (Claims Assessment &amp; Opinion)\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> QA Lead\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- All priority IRQs tested\n- Evidence levels assigned per METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md\n- Testing confidence rated (★1–★5)\n- No critical gaps in evidence (L1–L2 only on low-priority IRQs)\n- Test results peer-reviewed</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Phase 5: Claims Assessment &amp; Opinion</h2>\n<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Evaluate each core assurance claim against evidence and test results. Determine which claims are supported, which have conditions, which fail. Form independent assurance opinion (four-box conclusion).</p>\n<h3>Process</h3>\n<p><strong>Step 1: Claims-to-IRQ Mapping</strong>\n- Every core claim must be supported by ≥1 IRQ (two-way mapping per ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md mandatory completeness rule)\n- For each claim, list supporting IRQs and their test results</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Per-Claim Assessment</strong>\nFor each claim:\n- Did supporting IRQs pass testing? (Yes/No/Conditional)\n- Is evidence level sufficient? (L4–L5 on core claims for Tier 2–3)\n- Any testing confidence concerns? (Low ★1–★2 confidence?)\n- Residual risk: what risk remains after control validation?</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Materiality Screening</strong>\nPer ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md section 3 (10 materiality lenses):\n- Assess each finding against all 10 lenses\n- Material findings (material on ≥1 lens, per ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE's escalation rule): escalate to opinion\n- Non-material findings: relay to lifecycle monitoring</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Opinion Formation</strong>\nSelect one of four conclusions per OPINION_TEMPLATE.md:\n1. <strong>Should Proceed</strong> — No material deficiencies\n2. <strong>Should Proceed With Conditions</strong> — Conditions specified; must be met within timeline\n3. <strong>Should Not Proceed</strong> — Material deficiencies require remediation\n4. <strong>Unable to Form Opinion</strong> — Insufficient evidence</p>\n<p>For conditions, document:\n- Specific requirement (not vague)\n- Success criteria (testable)\n- Target date\n- Who verifies satisfaction</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5: Review Confidence Assessment</strong>\nAssign High/Medium/Low review confidence per ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md (an internal assessment, never issued as a grade; the issued report carries the statement on the basis of the review, OPINION_TEMPLATE.md Part G):\n- High = mature methods, L4–L5 evidence, ★4 tests\n- Medium = emerging techniques, mixed evidence\n- Low = frontier capability, ★1–★2 tests</p>\n<h3>Deliverable: Independent Assurance Opinion (Component 7)</h3>\n<p>Per OPINION_TEMPLATE.md:\n- Scope &amp; limitations\n- Assessment approach\n- Assurance claims summary table\n- Materiality-screened findings\n- Conditions (if any)\n- Four-box opinion\n- Statement on the basis of the review (Part G)\n- Regulatory alignment\n- Basis of opinion</p>\n<h3>Gate 5: Opinion Drafted &amp; Peer-Reviewed</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Present to sponsor\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> QA Lead + Assurance Lead\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- Opinion signed by authorized auditor (independence confirmed)\n- Claims-IRQ mapping complete (no orphans)\n- Conditions are specific and verifiable\n- Opinion conclusion is defensible (evidence supports conclusion)\n- Review confidence statement aligns with evidence levels and test confidence\n- <strong>The quality-control review was performed to a stated scope</strong>, covering each of: discussion of the significant matters with the engagement lead (the standard's engagement partner); review of the subject-matter information and the proposed report; review of selected engagement documentation relating to the significant judgements made and the conclusions reached; and evaluation of the conclusions reached in formulating the report and of whether the proposed report is appropriate to those conclusions. [Scope follows ISAE 3000 (Revised) para 36(b)(i)–(iv), pp.14–15; FAIAS applies it to every engagement, where the standard requires it only where law, regulation or the firm's own determination calls for a review.]\n- <strong>The reviewer was independent of the engagement team</strong> whose judgements are being evaluated, and that independence is recorded in the same terms as the signing auditor's.\n- <strong>The report is not dated before the review is complete.</strong> [ISAE 3000 (Revised) para 36(a).]\n- <strong>The reviewer's concurrence is recorded as an independent check, not as corroborating evidence for the conclusion.</strong> Agreement between the engagement team and the reviewer is evidence of consistency, not of correctness (ASSURANCE_MODEL.md §7A).</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Phase 6: Continuous Assurance &amp; Lifecycle Governance</h2>\n<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Establish post-deployment monitoring, define re-assessment triggers, and manage system through its operational lifecycle (Design → Build → Validate → Deploy → Monitor → Change → Retire).</p>\n<h3>Continuous Assurance Plan</h3>\n<p><strong>Early-Life Monitoring (Weeks 1–12 Post-Deployment)</strong>\nFor Tier 2–3 systems, weekly operational dashboards:\n- Key performance indicators (KPIs per claim)\n- Escalation alerts (thresholds that trigger incident response)\n- Override rate analysis (is human actually able to override?)\n- Monitoring data feeds into operational evidence (L5) for annual reassessment</p>\n<p><strong>Periodic Assurance (Annual Minimum)</strong>\n- Full reassessment for Tier 3 (highest risk)\n- Refresh assessment for Tier 2 (test subset of IRQs; review monitoring data)\n- Attestation for Tier 1\n- Policy review for Tier 0</p>\n<p><strong>Major-Change Re-Assessment Triggers</strong> (tied to IRQ-GOV-012 envelope drift):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Model Version Change</strong> — New base model or fine-tuning on new data → trigger re-test of model-domain IRQs (B series)</li>\n<li><strong>Training Data Change</strong> — New data source or rebalancing → trigger re-test of data IRQs (A series) + fairness IRQs (H series)</li>\n<li><strong>Decision Logic Change</strong> — Threshold adjustment, feature weighting change → trigger re-test of model + human oversight IRQs</li>\n<li><strong>Operating Envelope Expansion</strong> — New customer segment, new use case, increased autonomy → trigger full re-assessment (all domains)</li>\n<li><strong>Tool/API Access Change</strong> — New integrations, new external data sources → trigger security + third-party IRQs (E, F series)</li>\n<li><strong>Governance Change</strong> — Changed escalation procedure, changed accountability → trigger governance IRQs (I series)</li>\n<li><strong>Incident Triggering Material Finding</strong> — Incident revealing control failure → trigger immediate re-assessment of affected claims</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Lifecycle Phases:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Assurance Activities</th>\n<th>Gate Criteria</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Design</strong></td>\n<td>IRQ scoping, failure tree workshop</td>\n<td>Threat analysis complete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Build</strong></td>\n<td>Architecture review, control design validation</td>\n<td>Design meets assurance objectives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Validate</strong></td>\n<td>Phase 4–5 testing, evidence collection, opinion formation</td>\n<td>Opinion issued; conditions (if any) met</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deploy</strong></td>\n<td>Pre-go-live confirmation, monitoring setup</td>\n<td>Monitoring dashboard live</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monitor</strong></td>\n<td>Weekly KPI tracking, early-life analysis (weeks 1–12), escalation response</td>\n<td>KPIs within expected ranges; escalations managed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Change</strong></td>\n<td>Re-assessment trigger evaluation; if triggered, return to Scoping (Phase 2)</td>\n<td>Change approved against updated risk profile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Retire</strong></td>\n<td>Archival of evidence, lessons learned documentation</td>\n<td>System decommissioned; knowledge captured</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Deliverable: Continuous Assurance Plan</h3>\n<p>Document:\n- KPIs to monitor (per claim)\n- Escalation thresholds (what value triggers alert)\n- Monitoring cadence (weekly, monthly, quarterly)\n- Annual re-assessment schedule\n- Major-change escalation procedures\n- Roles (who monitors, who decides re-assessment need, who sponsors changes)\n- Contact information for assurance updates/re-engagement</p>\n<h3>Gate 6: Continuous Assurance &amp; Lifecycle Active</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Engagement closed (ongoing monitoring begins)\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> Operational owner (firm-side) + Assurance Lead\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- Monitoring dashboard implemented\n- KPI alerts configured\n- Escalation procedures documented\n- Annual re-assessment scheduled\n- All stakeholders briefed on re-assessment triggers</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Key Tools &amp; Artefacts per Phase</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Primary Artefacts</th>\n<th>Referenced FAIAS Components</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 1</strong></td>\n<td>AI Deployment Profile</td>\n<td>Intake checklist (new)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 2</strong></td>\n<td>Materiality Assessment Summary</td>\n<td>TIERING_MODEL.md</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 3</strong></td>\n<td>Failure Trees, Prioritised IRQ Set</td>\n<td>IRQ_TEMPLATE.md, ASSURANCE_MODEL.md (failure chains)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 4</strong></td>\n<td>Evidence Catalogue, Test Results</td>\n<td>Component 4, Component 5, METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md (evidence levels, testing confidence)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 5</strong></td>\n<td>Independent Assurance Opinion</td>\n<td>OPINION_TEMPLATE.md, ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md (claims, materiality)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 6</strong></td>\n<td>Continuous Assurance Plan, Monitoring Dashboard</td>\n<td>ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md (lifecycle)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>Governance &amp; Escalation</h2>\n<h3>Escalation Triggers (Stop &amp; Escalate to Sponsor)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Material control gaps that can't be remediated pre-deployment</li>\n<li>Inability to access test environment or critical evidence</li>\n<li>Regulatory feedback during engagement (change engagement scope)</li>\n<li>Sponsor conflict-of-interest (e.g., sponsor incentivized to downplay risk)</li>\n<li>Technical risk too novel to assess (insufficient research base)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Role Definitions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engagement Lead:</strong> Overall engagement delivery, sponsor communication, timeline management</li>\n<li><strong>Assurance Lead:</strong> Phases 2, 5; claims framework, opinion formation, review confidence</li>\n<li><strong>Testing Lead:</strong> Phase 4; test design, execution, evidence collection</li>\n<li><strong>Domain Expert:</strong> Phase 3; failure tree workshop; business risk interpretation</li>\n<li><strong>QA Lead:</strong> Quality gates, peer review of assessment and opinion</li>\n<li><strong>Sponsor:</strong> Business owner at the firm; receives the opinion and owns the deployment decision</li>\n<li><strong>Firm-side owners (Risk Architect, Operations, Operational owner):</strong> the firm's own staff who hold a named phase or gate jointly with the Assurance Lead; they are not part of the review team</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />","sections":[{"h":null,"html":"<h1>ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md — FAIAS Assurance Reviewer's Handbook</h1>\n<p><strong>Version:</strong> the release version in <code>README.md</code>; the signed manifest, <code>ownership/MANIFEST.sha256</code>, identifies the release.<br />\n<strong>Document Custodian:</strong> the maintainer</p>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Purpose","html":"<p>This document is the operational handbook for reviewers (assurance practitioners, internal audit, external auditors) conducting a FAIAS engagement. It defines six phases, deliverables per phase, governance decision-points, and tools to apply.</p>\n<p>A FAIAS engagement produces an Independent Assurance Opinion (Component 7) backed by systematic threat analysis, evidence collection, and claims assessment.</p>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Calibration note.</strong> Phase durations and procedure depth in this handbook describe the maximal, Tier 3 form of the engagement and assume manual execution. They are a ceiling to calibrate down from, not a required floor. Scope, depth, sample size and repetition are set per engagement against tier, consequence, evidence availability, the named expert's direction and the depth commissioned; automated test execution reduces effort on mechanical test classes substantially without changing the ★ confidence of the method. Every reduction is recorded, and anything not tested is dispositioned with its reason and consequence rather than omitted silently. See PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §5A.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"h":"FAIAS Engagement Model: Six Phases","html":"<pre><code>┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 1: Intake &amp; Scoping                           │\n│ Output: AI Deployment Profile (1-pager)             │\n│ Owner: Engagement Lead + Domain Expert              │\n│ Duration: 1–2 weeks                                 │\n│ Gate: Scope validated; AI Risk Tier tentative       │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 2: Materiality Assessment                     │\n│ Output: AI Risk Tier + Scoped IRQ Set               │\n│ Owner: Assurance Lead + Risk Architect (firm-side)  │\n│ Duration: 1 week                                    │\n│ Gate: Tier confirmed; engagement scope locked       │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 3: Threat &amp; Failure Analysis                 │\n│ Output: Prioritised IRQ Set + Failure Trees         │\n│ Owner: Joint workshop (domain expert + AI reviewer)  │\n│ Duration: 2–3 weeks                                 │\n│ Gate: Failure modes validated; IRQ list finalised   │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 4: Evidence &amp; Testing                         │\n│ Output: Test Results, Evidence Catalogue            │\n│ Owner: Testing Lead                                 │\n│ Duration: 4–9 weeks (depends on Tier; §6 stages)    │\n│ Gate: Testing complete; evidence levels assigned    │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 5: Claims Assessment &amp; Opinion                │\n│ Output: Independent Assurance Opinion               │\n│ Owner: Assurance Lead + Engagement Lead             │\n│ Duration: 2–3 weeks                                 │\n│ Gate: Opinion reviewed by QA; issued to the sponsor │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Phase 6: Continuous Assurance &amp; Lifecycle           │\n│ Output: Monitoring Plan, Re-Assessment Schedule     │\n│ Owner: Assurance Lead + Operations (firm-side)      │\n│ Duration: Ongoing (design through retirement)       │\n│ Gate: Monitoring activated; lifecycle documented    │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n</code></pre>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Phase 1: Intake & Scoping","html":"<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Gather information about the AI system, its deployment context, and business/regulatory environment. Produce a one-page AI Deployment Profile for sponsor review.</p>\n<h3>Information Gathered</h3>\n<h4>Business Context</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use Case &amp; Business Objective:</strong> What decision or task does the AI support? What customer outcome is enabled? (e.g., \"Support credit underwriting decision for SME lending\")</li>\n<li><strong>Process &amp; Workflow:</strong> How does the AI output integrate into business process? Who reviews the AI output? Who is accountable for the decision? Is there a human-in-loop or is system autonomous?</li>\n<li><strong>Customer Impact:</strong> Which customers are affected? What's the materiality of the decision (eligibility, pricing, access to service)? Are vulnerable customers involved (disability, elderly, financial literacy)?</li>\n<li><strong>Regulatory Obligations:</strong> Which regulatory frameworks apply? (FCA SYSC 4.1, PRA model risk, Consumer Duty, AML/CFT, data protection, market conduct, etc.)</li>\n<li><strong>Accountable Executive:</strong> Who owns this system end-to-end? Who is the SMCR-accountable person for model risk governance?</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>AI System Context</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Model Type &amp; Provenance:</strong> Is this a large language model (LLM), supervised classifier, regression model, reinforcement learning agent, ensemble? Who built/trained it (internal, external vendor)? What version?</li>\n<li><strong>Fine-Tuning or Adaptation:</strong> Has the base model been fine-tuned? On what data? How recently? Who owns fine-tuning decisions?</li>\n<li><strong>Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG):</strong> If system uses retrieval, what are the data sources? How fresh are they? Who curates them?</li>\n<li><strong>Agents &amp; Tool Use:</strong> Does system have access to tools, APIs, or autonomous decision-making? What are the tools/decisions it can make?</li>\n<li><strong>Memory &amp; State:</strong> Does system maintain context across multiple requests? Is state persisted? Are there prompt injections/jailbreak risks?</li>\n<li><strong>Human Involvement:</strong> What does human reviewer do? Can human override? Is override logged and audited?</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Deployment Environment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Development Environment:</strong> Where is the system built? Access controls, version control, testing protocols</li>\n<li><strong>Test/Staging Environment:</strong> Is there a non-production environment? How representative is it of production?</li>\n<li><strong>Production Environment:</strong> Cloud provider? Self-hosted? Data residency? Incident response procedures?</li>\n<li><strong>Third-Party Dependencies:</strong> Model host (e.g., OpenAI API), data providers, infrastructure vendors. Who has data access? What are SLAs?</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring &amp; Alerting:</strong> What KPIs are tracked? Are there automated alerts? Who responds to incidents?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Deliverable: AI Deployment Profile (1-pager)</h3>\n<p><strong>Template:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nAI DEPLOYMENT PROFILE\nSystem: [Name]  |  Version: [X.Y]  |  Date: [YYYY-MM-DD]\n═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nBUSINESS CONTEXT\nUse Case: [One-sentence: what problem does it solve?]\nDecision Supported: [Who decides? Human or autonomous?]\nCustomer Impact: [Materiality: eligibility / pricing / access / none]\nRegulatory Framework: [FCA, PRA, GDPR, etc.]\nAccountable Executive: [Name, Title]\n\nAI SYSTEM\nModel Type: [LLM / classifier / agent / ensemble / other]\nProvider: [Internal / OpenAI / Anthropic / other]\nAdaptation: [Fine-tuned? RAG? Custom tools?]\nHuman Role: [Advisory / approver / exception handler / post-audit]\n\nDEPLOYMENT ENVIRONMENT\nStaging: [Yes/No — representative of production?]\nProduction: [Cloud provider / data residency]\nMonitoring: [KPIs tracked; alerting in place?]\nIncidents: [Last 12 months: [count] incidents; escalation procedure documented]\n\nPRELIMINARY RISK TIER (to be confirmed in Phase 2):\n[ ] Tier 0  [ ] Tier 1  [ ] Tier 2  [ ] Tier 3\n\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS FOR PHASE 2:\n1. [Domain expert question e.g., &quot;Are vulnerable customers affected?&quot;]\n2. [Reviewer question e.g., &quot;Is RAG data source controlled?&quot;]\n3. [Regulatory question e.g., &quot;Does PRA model risk governance apply?&quot;]\n\nPrepared by: [Name]  |  Sponsor Review: [ ] Approved\n═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Gate 1: Intake Complete</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Proceed to Phase 2 (Materiality Assessment)\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> Sponsor + Engagement Lead\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- Deployment Profile complete and sponsor-reviewed\n- Critical questions identified\n- No blockers to proceeding (e.g., no \"system not yet built\" or \"scope undefined\")</p>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Phase 2: Materiality Assessment","html":"<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Apply TIERING_MODEL.md to determine AI Risk Tier. Tier determines scope of IRQ assessment, evidence levels, opinion type, and engagement duration.</p>\n<h3>Process</h3>\n<p><strong>Step 1: Score Five Factors</strong>\n- Customer Impact (1–4)\n- Financial Impact (1–4)\n- Regulatory Impact (1–4)\n- Autonomy Level (0–5, normalized to 1–4)\n- System Complexity (0–5, normalized to 1–4)</p>\n<p>Use TIERING_MODEL.md definitions and worked examples; involve domain expert to ground scoring.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Calculate Composite Risk Score</strong></p>\n<pre><code>Risk Score = \n  (Customer Impact × 0.25) + \n  (Financial Impact × 0.25) + \n  (Regulatory Impact × 0.25) + \n  (Autonomy normalized × 0.125) + \n  (Complexity normalized × 0.125)\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Check Override Triggers</strong>\n- Vulnerable customer exposure? Bump up 1 tier.\n- Regulatory feedback history? Bump up 1 tier.\n- Emerging technology risk? Bump up 1 tier.\n- Other? (See TIERING_MODEL.md section: Override Triggers)</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Assign Tier</strong>\nMap Risk Score to tier (Tier 0–3 per TIERING_MODEL.md).</p>\n<h3>Deliverable: Materiality Assessment Summary</h3>\n<p>Document:\n- Five-factor scores with rationale per factor\n- Composite Risk Score calculation\n- Override conditions (if any)\n- Final Tier assignment\n- Scope of IRQ assessment (which domains in scope)\n- Engagement duration estimate\n- Evidence-level minimums per tier</p>\n<h3>Gate 2: Materiality Assessment &amp; Tier Locked</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Proceed to Phase 3 (Threat &amp; Failure Analysis)\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> Assurance Lead (or Sponsor for urgent engagements)\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- Tier assignment justified and documented\n- All five factors scored with rationale\n- Override triggers reviewed\n- Scope of IRQs locked (no future scope changes without re-tiering)\n- Engagement plan (duration, team, budget) approved</p>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Phase 3: Threat & Failure Analysis","html":"<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Conduct a joint workshop with domain expert (business owner, process subject-matter expert) and AI reviewer to build failure trees: Business Objective → AI Function → AI Behaviour → Failure Modes → Consequences. This grounds IRQ prioritisation in business risk, not technical curiosity.</p>\n<h3>Workshop Structure</h3>\n<p><strong>Part A: Business Objectives (Domain Expert)</strong>\n- \"What are the 5–10 key decisions this process makes?\"\n- \"What are the worst outcomes if a decision is wrong?\"\n- \"Which decisions are most material to the business?\"\n- Example: Lending: \"Decisions: approve/decline/tier. Worst outcome: approve bad credit (loss). Material: yes.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Part B: AI Functions (AI Reviewer)</strong>\n- \"What does the AI do in the workflow?\"\n- \"What does the AI output look like? (score, classification, ranking, recommendation)\"\n- \"How does human use the output?\" (override, approve, exception-handle, accept as-is)\n- Example: \"System outputs credit score 650–850. Underwriter approves if &gt;700.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Part C: AI Behaviours (Joint)</strong>\n- \"What could the AI do differently than expected?\"\n- \"Are there edge cases where behaviour diverges from training?\"\n- Example: \"System hallucinates customer data from similar names. System lacks context on recent life events (job loss, bankruptcy).\"</p>\n<p><strong>Part D: Failure Modes (AI Reviewer)</strong>\n- \"How does behaviour cross the unsafe boundary?\"\n- \"What are the ways confidence becomes unsafe?\"\n- Example: \"System confidently recommends approval for customer with recent CCJ (confident hallucination of creditworthiness).\"</p>\n<p><strong>Part E: Consequences (Domain Expert)</strong>\n- \"What is the business/customer/regulatory impact?\"\n- Example: \"Wrong approval → customer in unaffordable debt → regulatory breach (affordability) → FOS complaint → capital charge.\"</p>\n<h3>Deliverable: Failure Trees (Per Business Objective)</h3>\n<p><strong>Template:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>BUSINESS OBJECTIVE: Approve/decline credit for SME lending\n│\n├─ DECISION: Approve if system score &gt;750\n│  │\n│  ├─ AI BEHAVIOUR: System scores applicant based on financial history\n│  │  │\n│  │  ├─ FAILURE MODE: System hallucinates customer creditworthiness\n│  │  │                (confident score based on fabricated data)\n│  │  │  └─ CONSEQUENCE: Approve unaffordable loan → customer default\n│  │  │                  → regulatory breach → FOS complaint\n│  │  │                  → Tier: Customer impact 4, Financial 4, Regulatory 4\n│  │  │                  → IRQs: IRQ-MOD-004 (hallucination), IRQ-DAT-003 (data quality),\n│  │  │                          IRQ-HUM-004 (escalation), IRQ-CON-005 (financial exclusion)\n│  │  │\n│  │  └─ FAILURE MODE: System confidence miscalibrated\n│  │                   (score high but reliability low)\n│  │     └─ CONSEQUENCE: Approve borderline credit → customer struggle\n│  │                     → complaint → reputation damage\n│  │                     → IRQs: IRQ-MOD-006 (confidence calibration)\n│  │\n│  └─ FAILURE MODE: System over-relies on single weak signal\n│                   (e.g., recent industry switch)\n│     └─ CONSEQUENCE: Approve risky credit → loss → capital impact\n│                     → IRQs: IRQ-DAT-006 (ingestion and curation integrity), IRQ-MOD-010 (robustness to shift)\n│\n└─ DECISION: Decline if system score &lt;700\n   │\n   └─ FAILURE MODE: System cannot adapt to changed circumstances\n                    (customer recovering after historic default)\n      └─ CONSEQUENCE: Decline creditworthy applicant → customer friction\n                      → regulatory fairness concern (opportunity)\n                      → IRQs: IRQ-DAT-007 (retrieval currency), IRQ-CON-003 (fairness)\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Phase 3 Prioritisation</h3>\n<p>For each failure mode, assign <strong>priority</strong> based on:\n1. <strong>Severity:</strong> Impact on customer/firm (high/medium/low)\n2. <strong>Likelihood:</strong> How easily could this happen in production? (high/medium/low)\n3. <strong>Detectability:</strong> How easy is it to catch (with current controls)? (easy/medium/hard)</p>\n<p><strong>Priority = Severity + Likelihood − Detectability</strong> (conceptually; use FAIAS 5-axis risk weighting if finer granularity needed)</p>\n<h3>Deliverable: Prioritised IRQ Set</h3>\n<p><strong>Output:</strong>\n- List of IRQs in scope for this engagement (selected from the full FAIAS battery — 109 IRQs as at v1.2.0; see IRQ_REGISTER.md)\n- IRQs ranked by priority (high → medium → low)\n- Cross-domain chains identified (e.g., data poisoning → model hallucination → human accept → customer loss)</p>\n<h3>Gate 3: Failure Analysis &amp; IRQ Set Locked</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Proceed to Phase 4 (Evidence &amp; Testing)\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> Domain Expert + Assurance Lead\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- Failure trees documented (business objective → failure mode → consequence)\n- All critical failure modes identified\n- IRQ set prioritised\n- Cross-domain chains documented\n- No unaddressed failure modes</p>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Phase 4: Evidence & Testing","html":"<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Execute non-production testing per Component 5 Testing Playbook, collect evidence per Component 4 Evidence Catalogue, and assign evidence levels (L1–L5) and testing confidence (★1–★5).</p>\n<h3>Process per Tier</h3>\n<p><strong>Tier 0:</strong>\n- Design review only (no testing)\n- Governance documentation audit (policy, roles)\n- Evidence level: L2</p>\n<p><strong>Tier 1:</strong>\n- Staged testing for priority IRQs (not exhaustive)\n- Evidence: L3 (design + demo)\n- Testing confidence: ★3–★4 where available; ★1–★2 procedures run with disclosure (PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §8)</p>\n<p><strong>Tier 2:</strong>\n- Comprehensive staged testing (all IRQs in scope)\n- Benchmarked test methods (OWASP, NIST)\n- Evidence: L4 (independent testing)\n- Testing confidence: ★4 or above on material tests for reasonable assurance; a material test rated ★1–★3 runs with disclosure, and the opinion is then given at limited assurance</p>\n<p><strong>Tier 3:</strong>\n- Exhaustive staged testing (all IRQs, multiple tests per IRQ)\n- L4 evidence from staging\n- L5 evidence post-deployment (operational validation)\n- Testing confidence: ★4 or above on all material tests for reasonable assurance; otherwise as Tier 2</p>\n<h3>Deliverable: Evidence Catalogue (Component 4)</h3>\n<p>Structured inventory of all evidence collected, per FAIAS template:\n- Policy &amp; governance documents\n- Technical design\n- Testing results (per IRQ, per test)\n- Monitoring data\n- Third-party attestations</p>\n<p><strong>Evidence tagging:</strong>\n- L1 (assertion), L2 (documentation), L3 (demo), L4 (independent test), L5 (operational)\n- Confidence rating (★1–★5)\n- Which claim(s) does evidence support?</p>\n<h3>Gate 4: Testing Complete &amp; Evidence Catalogued</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Proceed to Phase 5 (Claims Assessment &amp; Opinion)\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> QA Lead\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- All priority IRQs tested\n- Evidence levels assigned per METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md\n- Testing confidence rated (★1–★5)\n- No critical gaps in evidence (L1–L2 only on low-priority IRQs)\n- Test results peer-reviewed</p>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Phase 5: Claims Assessment & Opinion","html":"<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Evaluate each core assurance claim against evidence and test results. Determine which claims are supported, which have conditions, which fail. Form independent assurance opinion (four-box conclusion).</p>\n<h3>Process</h3>\n<p><strong>Step 1: Claims-to-IRQ Mapping</strong>\n- Every core claim must be supported by ≥1 IRQ (two-way mapping per ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md mandatory completeness rule)\n- For each claim, list supporting IRQs and their test results</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Per-Claim Assessment</strong>\nFor each claim:\n- Did supporting IRQs pass testing? (Yes/No/Conditional)\n- Is evidence level sufficient? (L4–L5 on core claims for Tier 2–3)\n- Any testing confidence concerns? (Low ★1–★2 confidence?)\n- Residual risk: what risk remains after control validation?</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Materiality Screening</strong>\nPer ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md section 3 (10 materiality lenses):\n- Assess each finding against all 10 lenses\n- Material findings (material on ≥1 lens, per ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE's escalation rule): escalate to opinion\n- Non-material findings: relay to lifecycle monitoring</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Opinion Formation</strong>\nSelect one of four conclusions per OPINION_TEMPLATE.md:\n1. <strong>Should Proceed</strong> — No material deficiencies\n2. <strong>Should Proceed With Conditions</strong> — Conditions specified; must be met within timeline\n3. <strong>Should Not Proceed</strong> — Material deficiencies require remediation\n4. <strong>Unable to Form Opinion</strong> — Insufficient evidence</p>\n<p>For conditions, document:\n- Specific requirement (not vague)\n- Success criteria (testable)\n- Target date\n- Who verifies satisfaction</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5: Review Confidence Assessment</strong>\nAssign High/Medium/Low review confidence per ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md (an internal assessment, never issued as a grade; the issued report carries the statement on the basis of the review, OPINION_TEMPLATE.md Part G):\n- High = mature methods, L4–L5 evidence, ★4 tests\n- Medium = emerging techniques, mixed evidence\n- Low = frontier capability, ★1–★2 tests</p>\n<h3>Deliverable: Independent Assurance Opinion (Component 7)</h3>\n<p>Per OPINION_TEMPLATE.md:\n- Scope &amp; limitations\n- Assessment approach\n- Assurance claims summary table\n- Materiality-screened findings\n- Conditions (if any)\n- Four-box opinion\n- Statement on the basis of the review (Part G)\n- Regulatory alignment\n- Basis of opinion</p>\n<h3>Gate 5: Opinion Drafted &amp; Peer-Reviewed</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Present to sponsor\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> QA Lead + Assurance Lead\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- Opinion signed by authorized auditor (independence confirmed)\n- Claims-IRQ mapping complete (no orphans)\n- Conditions are specific and verifiable\n- Opinion conclusion is defensible (evidence supports conclusion)\n- Review confidence statement aligns with evidence levels and test confidence\n- <strong>The quality-control review was performed to a stated scope</strong>, covering each of: discussion of the significant matters with the engagement lead (the standard's engagement partner); review of the subject-matter information and the proposed report; review of selected engagement documentation relating to the significant judgements made and the conclusions reached; and evaluation of the conclusions reached in formulating the report and of whether the proposed report is appropriate to those conclusions. [Scope follows ISAE 3000 (Revised) para 36(b)(i)–(iv), pp.14–15; FAIAS applies it to every engagement, where the standard requires it only where law, regulation or the firm's own determination calls for a review.]\n- <strong>The reviewer was independent of the engagement team</strong> whose judgements are being evaluated, and that independence is recorded in the same terms as the signing auditor's.\n- <strong>The report is not dated before the review is complete.</strong> [ISAE 3000 (Revised) para 36(a).]\n- <strong>The reviewer's concurrence is recorded as an independent check, not as corroborating evidence for the conclusion.</strong> Agreement between the engagement team and the reviewer is evidence of consistency, not of correctness (ASSURANCE_MODEL.md §7A).</p>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Phase 6: Continuous Assurance & Lifecycle Governance","html":"<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Establish post-deployment monitoring, define re-assessment triggers, and manage system through its operational lifecycle (Design → Build → Validate → Deploy → Monitor → Change → Retire).</p>\n<h3>Continuous Assurance Plan</h3>\n<p><strong>Early-Life Monitoring (Weeks 1–12 Post-Deployment)</strong>\nFor Tier 2–3 systems, weekly operational dashboards:\n- Key performance indicators (KPIs per claim)\n- Escalation alerts (thresholds that trigger incident response)\n- Override rate analysis (is human actually able to override?)\n- Monitoring data feeds into operational evidence (L5) for annual reassessment</p>\n<p><strong>Periodic Assurance (Annual Minimum)</strong>\n- Full reassessment for Tier 3 (highest risk)\n- Refresh assessment for Tier 2 (test subset of IRQs; review monitoring data)\n- Attestation for Tier 1\n- Policy review for Tier 0</p>\n<p><strong>Major-Change Re-Assessment Triggers</strong> (tied to IRQ-GOV-012 envelope drift):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Model Version Change</strong> — New base model or fine-tuning on new data → trigger re-test of model-domain IRQs (B series)</li>\n<li><strong>Training Data Change</strong> — New data source or rebalancing → trigger re-test of data IRQs (A series) + fairness IRQs (H series)</li>\n<li><strong>Decision Logic Change</strong> — Threshold adjustment, feature weighting change → trigger re-test of model + human oversight IRQs</li>\n<li><strong>Operating Envelope Expansion</strong> — New customer segment, new use case, increased autonomy → trigger full re-assessment (all domains)</li>\n<li><strong>Tool/API Access Change</strong> — New integrations, new external data sources → trigger security + third-party IRQs (E, F series)</li>\n<li><strong>Governance Change</strong> — Changed escalation procedure, changed accountability → trigger governance IRQs (I series)</li>\n<li><strong>Incident Triggering Material Finding</strong> — Incident revealing control failure → trigger immediate re-assessment of affected claims</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Lifecycle Phases:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Assurance Activities</th>\n<th>Gate Criteria</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Design</strong></td>\n<td>IRQ scoping, failure tree workshop</td>\n<td>Threat analysis complete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Build</strong></td>\n<td>Architecture review, control design validation</td>\n<td>Design meets assurance objectives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Validate</strong></td>\n<td>Phase 4–5 testing, evidence collection, opinion formation</td>\n<td>Opinion issued; conditions (if any) met</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deploy</strong></td>\n<td>Pre-go-live confirmation, monitoring setup</td>\n<td>Monitoring dashboard live</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monitor</strong></td>\n<td>Weekly KPI tracking, early-life analysis (weeks 1–12), escalation response</td>\n<td>KPIs within expected ranges; escalations managed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Change</strong></td>\n<td>Re-assessment trigger evaluation; if triggered, return to Scoping (Phase 2)</td>\n<td>Change approved against updated risk profile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Retire</strong></td>\n<td>Archival of evidence, lessons learned documentation</td>\n<td>System decommissioned; knowledge captured</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Deliverable: Continuous Assurance Plan</h3>\n<p>Document:\n- KPIs to monitor (per claim)\n- Escalation thresholds (what value triggers alert)\n- Monitoring cadence (weekly, monthly, quarterly)\n- Annual re-assessment schedule\n- Major-change escalation procedures\n- Roles (who monitors, who decides re-assessment need, who sponsors changes)\n- Contact information for assurance updates/re-engagement</p>\n<h3>Gate 6: Continuous Assurance &amp; Lifecycle Active</h3>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Engagement closed (ongoing monitoring begins)\n<strong>Gate Owner:</strong> Operational owner (firm-side) + Assurance Lead\n<strong>Criteria:</strong>\n- Monitoring dashboard implemented\n- KPI alerts configured\n- Escalation procedures documented\n- Annual re-assessment scheduled\n- All stakeholders briefed on re-assessment triggers</p>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Key Tools & Artefacts per Phase","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Primary Artefacts</th>\n<th>Referenced FAIAS Components</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 1</strong></td>\n<td>AI Deployment Profile</td>\n<td>Intake checklist (new)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 2</strong></td>\n<td>Materiality Assessment Summary</td>\n<td>TIERING_MODEL.md</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 3</strong></td>\n<td>Failure Trees, Prioritised IRQ Set</td>\n<td>IRQ_TEMPLATE.md, ASSURANCE_MODEL.md (failure chains)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 4</strong></td>\n<td>Evidence Catalogue, Test Results</td>\n<td>Component 4, Component 5, METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md (evidence levels, testing confidence)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 5</strong></td>\n<td>Independent Assurance Opinion</td>\n<td>OPINION_TEMPLATE.md, ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md (claims, materiality)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase 6</strong></td>\n<td>Continuous Assurance Plan, Monitoring Dashboard</td>\n<td>ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md (lifecycle)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Governance & Escalation","html":"<h3>Escalation Triggers (Stop &amp; Escalate to Sponsor)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Material control gaps that can't be remediated pre-deployment</li>\n<li>Inability to access test environment or critical evidence</li>\n<li>Regulatory feedback during engagement (change engagement scope)</li>\n<li>Sponsor conflict-of-interest (e.g., sponsor incentivized to downplay risk)</li>\n<li>Technical risk too novel to assess (insufficient research base)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Role Definitions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engagement Lead:</strong> Overall engagement delivery, sponsor communication, timeline management</li>\n<li><strong>Assurance Lead:</strong> Phases 2, 5; claims framework, opinion formation, review confidence</li>\n<li><strong>Testing Lead:</strong> Phase 4; test design, execution, evidence collection</li>\n<li><strong>Domain Expert:</strong> Phase 3; failure tree workshop; business risk interpretation</li>\n<li><strong>QA Lead:</strong> Quality gates, peer review of assessment and opinion</li>\n<li><strong>Sponsor:</strong> Business owner at the firm; receives the opinion and owns the deployment decision</li>\n<li><strong>Firm-side owners (Risk Architect, Operations, Operational owner):</strong> the firm's own staff who hold a named phase or gate jointly with the Assurance Lead; they are not part of the review team</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />"}]};
